From: WFI-048 follow-up -- the boundary class denied the repository's own prose corpus
Subject: [PATCH] fix(guard): drop the backtick from the embedded-path boundary class

Human-applied patch. All five targets are protected gate artifacts.

tests/guard-staging-exemption.tests.sh asserts that a commit message naming a
protected path inside backticks stays ALLOWED. With the backtick in the
boundary class both edges of the path abut a delimiter, so the embedded-path
pass matched and the command was denied on all three runtimes. That suite is
exactly the false-positive corpus WFI-048's Verification Plan step 4 named; it
existed already and was not run before the class was chosen.

Removing the backtick costs no true positive: every embedded-path shape in the
corpus wraps its path in a single or double quote. The repository's prose cases
are now part of the WFI-048 corpus itself, so the next boundary change is
measured against them.

The three guard twins are unchanged -- the class lives in the canonical JSON
and reaches them through the generated modules.

Apply WHOLE, then regenerate-check:
    git apply --check reports/notes/wfi-048c-boundary-backtick.patch
    git apply reports/notes/wfi-048c-boundary-backtick.patch
    python3 plugins/sdd-quality-loop/scripts/generate-guard-invariants.py --check
    bash tests/guard-staging-exemption.tests.sh

